│ marker test)(Haddow et al., 1992). In Maine, prenatally detected Down syndrome cases increased from 49% in 1986–1990 │
│ to 57% in 1991–1993 when triple marker tests replaced AFP serum screening (Palomaki and Beauregard, 1996). A similar │
│ increase was observed in New York State between 1984–1989 and 1990–1993 (Olsen and Cross, 1997). The subsequent      │
│ addition of inhibin-A as a fourth marker, together with maternal age (the quad marker test), improved the detection  │
│ rate to 80% when a 5% false-positive rate was employed. The proportion of pregnancies screened has also              │
│ approximately doubled since 1988 (Olsen and Cross, 1997; Palomaki et al., 1993). These events were expected to       │
│ increase the number of prenatally diagnosed Down syndrome cases. To evaluate the impact of maternal serum screening  │
